.\" fsb(1): manual page. Hand-written roff (man(7) macros).
.\" Check with:  mandoc -Tlint man/fsb.1   and   man ./man/fsb.1
.TH FSB 1 "September 21, 2026" "fsb 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fsb \- filesystem browser: a read-only, local-only web view of your files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsb
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-open ]
.RB [ \-\-browser
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-system\-root ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR PATH ]...
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B fsb \-\-init
.br
.B fsb \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsb
serves a web interface for browsing your own files on macOS.
It listens on the loopback address
.B 127.0.0.1
only, prints a single-use address, opens it in your browser, and runs until you
interrupt it.
In the browser you can list folders, sort, filter and search by name, and
preview
files (text and source code, JSON, CSV, Markdown, images, PDFs, and the
contents of
zip and tar archives) without leaving the window.
.PP
.B fsb
is read-only.
It never modifies, creates, renames, moves or deletes anything it serves.
The only file it ever creates is by
.BR \-\-init ,
which writes two new rule files and never overwrites one.
.PP
Two rule files control what you see.
Paths matching the
.I ignore
rules are hidden from listings and search but remain reachable by their path.
Paths matching the
.I deny
rules are never served in any way, and are answered exactly as a path that
does not
exist.
A built-in set of deny rules covers known credential and secret locations, and
is
active even if you have no rule files.
.PP
Only the folders you name are served.
With no path,
.B fsb
serves your home folder.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be written with one or two leading hyphens.
.TP
.I path
The first root to serve.
It must be an existing folder.
At most one is accepted.
The default is your home folder.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
Serve an additional root, such as
.BR /Volumes/Data .
May be repeated.
When there are several roots the interface offers a way to switch between them.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
Listen on port
.I N
instead of a random free port picked at each start.
The browser keeps preferences such as column widths and the preview pane's
width per address, including the port, so a random port each start means a
fresh, empty preference store every time; a fixed port keeps them across
restarts.
Must be 0\-65535; 0 (the default) picks a free port.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-system\-root
Permit
.B /
as a root.
Without this option
.B fsb
refuses to serve
.BR / .
The rules still apply.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
Print the launch URL and do not open a browser.
Use this to paste the URL into a browser of your choice.
.TP
.BI \-\-browser " NAME"
Open the launch URL in the macOS application
.I NAME
(for example
.BR """Google Chrome""" )
instead of your default browser.
A name beginning with a hyphen, or containing a NUL, carriage return or
newline, is
refused.
If the application cannot be opened,
.B fsb
says so and keeps running; the printed URL still works.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log verbosely to standard error, and answer a request for a denied path with a
body
naming the rule that matched (the status is still 404).
Nothing else reveals which rule matched.
.TP
.B \-\-init
Write the default rule files to
.I ~/.config/fsb/
and exit.
The folder is created with mode 0700 and each file with mode 0600.
A file that already exists is left unchanged and reported.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit with status 0.
.SH STARTING AND STOPPING
On success
.B fsb
prints, on standard output, a line naming the roots and the address, then the
launch
URL:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb: serving /Users/you read-only on http://127.0.0.1:53349
fsb: open this single-use URL: http://127.0.0.1:53349/AaGF.../?token=...
.fi
.RE
.PP
The port is chosen at random at each start and is not configurable.
The URL works once: the first request that uses it sets a session cookie in your
browser and the token is then useless, so a copy left in your shell history or
browser
history cannot be used.
It also contains a random path prefix under which everything is served; a
bookmark of
the URL does not survive a restart.
.PP
.B fsb
runs in the foreground.
Interrupt it with Control-C or send
.BR SIGTERM ;
it stops accepting requests, allows requests in progress up to three seconds to
finish, and exits with status 0.
.SH USING THE INTERFACE
.SS Keys
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " PgUp ", " PgDn ", " Home ", " End
Move the selection.
.TP
.BR Enter " or " Right
Open the selected folder.
A search result opens in its folder with the file selected.
.TP
.BR Left " or " Backspace
Go up one folder, reselecting the one you left.
.TP
.B Space
Show or hide the preview pane.
.TP
.B /
Filter this folder by name.
.TP
.B s
Search subfolders by name.
Enter runs the search.
.TP
.B g
Go to a path.
Accepts an absolute path or one beginning with
.BR ~/ .
.TP
.B c
Copy the selected entry's path (the folder's, if nothing is selected).
.TP
.B Escape
Clear the search, close a menu, or leave a text box.
.TP
.BR Alt+Left ", " Alt+Right
With a column header focused, move the column.
.PP
Shortcuts do not act while you are typing in a text box.
.SS Options in the window
.B Hidden files
shows names that begin with a dot (it never reveals an ignored or denied entry).
.B Folders first
lists folders before files under every sort column.
.B Match case
makes the filter and search case-sensitive.
.B Hover previews
shows the first lines of a file when you hold the pointer over it.
.B Preview pane
shows or hides the pane.
Column widths, order and visibility, and these choices, are remembered by the
browser,
never on the server or on disk.
.SS Names
Two names are the same name when the file system says so: on the default
case-insensitive macOS volume, when they are equal after Unicode normalization
and full
case folding (so that the sharp s and
.BR ss ,
or a ligature and its letters, are the same).
The filter, search and every rule follow this.
A name that contains characters that would disguise it, such as a right-to-left
override or a newline, is shown with a visible marker such as
.BR <U+202E> .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/ignore
Hide rules.
If absent, the built-in rules apply:
.BR .DS_Store ,
.B .git/
and
.BR node_modules/ .
.TP
.I ~/.config/fsb/deny
Block rules.
If absent, the core deny rules below apply.
If present it is authoritative: a core rule that it lacks is not enforced, and
.B fsb
warns about it.
.PP
Both files are read once, at startup, and are global; there are no per-folder
rule
files.
A file that cannot be parsed stops
.B fsb
from starting.
.SH RULE SYNTAX
The syntax is that of
.BR gitignore (5).
A line is blank, a comment (first character
.BR # ),
or a rule.
Surrounding white space is ignored, and a leading byte order mark and any line
ending
are accepted.
.TP
.B *
Any characters except
.BR / .
.TP
.B ?
One character except
.BR / .
.TP
.BR [abc] ", " [!abc]
A character class (which never matches
.BR / ).
.TP
.B **
Any number of folders:
.B **/name
matches at any depth.
.TP
.B trailing /
The rule applies to folders only.
.TP
.B \e#
A literal
.BR # .
.TP
.B !
Negation, allowed in the ignore file only.
.PP
A pattern with no slash matches a name at any depth.
A pattern beginning
.B ~/
is relative to your home folder; one beginning with
.B /
is absolute; one containing a slash elsewhere is relative to your home folder.
Matching ignores letter case and normalization as described under
.BR Names ,
and ignores trailing white space in a name.
.PP
An excluded folder excludes everything in it.
Deny wins over ignore, and nothing in the ignore file can expose a denied path.
.PP
A rule file must work as written or fail loudly.
.B fsb
refuses to start, naming the file, line and problem, for negation in the deny
file, an
empty pattern, a trailing backslash, a
.B /
inside
.BR [...] ,
.BI ~ name
(another user's home), a pattern in quotes, an inline comment (write
.B \e#
for a literal
.BR # ),
an invisible or formatting character, and a character that looks like a slash
but is
not one.
.SH DEFAULT DENY RULES
These core rules are built in and match at any depth, so the same folders in a
backup,
in a clone of your home folder or in another account are protected too:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&.ssh/  .aws/  .gnupg/  .kube/
**/.config/gh/
**/Library/Keychains/
**/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/
**/Library/Application Support/Firefox/
**/Library/Safari/  **/Library/Cookies/
\&.netrc  .git\-credentials  .pgpass
\&.env  .env.*  *.pem  *.key
id_rsa  id_dsa  id_ecdsa  id_ed25519  id_ecdsa_sk  id_ed25519_sk
*.p12  *.pfx  *.ppk  *.jks  *.keystore
*.kdbx  *.keychain  *.keychain\-db
.fi
.RE
.PP
Public key files such as
.B id_rsa.pub
are not denied.
The secret-file patterns are deliberately broad:
.B *.key
also matches Keynote presentations,
.B .env
also matches a folder of that name (such as a Python virtual environment), and
.B .env.*
also matches templates such as
.BR .env.example .
If you need one of them, remove the line from your deny file; you will get the
warning
below.
.PP
Optional rules, commented out in the file
.B \-\-init
writes, are
.BR ~/.docker/config.json ,
.BR ~/.npmrc ,
.B ~/Library/Mail/
and
.BR ~/Library/Messages/ .
.PP
If your deny file lacks a core rule, at startup
.B fsb
prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb: warning: core deny rule(s) disabled: .ssh/, .aws/; these paths are browsable.
.fi
.RE
.PP
and the interface shows a banner with the same message until every core rule
is back in
effect.
This cannot be turned off.
.PP
A file with several names (a hard link) is judged by its identity: if any name
is
denied, every name is refused and unlisted.
The names are found by an index built in the background at startup (about 20
seconds
for a home folder of 700,000 files); until it is ready, files that have
several names
are refused.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Your home folder.
It is resolved to its real location and determines the default root, the
location of
the configuration folder, and the meaning of
.B ~
in rules.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal shutdown, or
.BR \-\-help ,
or
.BR \-\-init .
.TP
.B 1
Any error, reported on standard error as one line beginning
.BR fsb: .
No server is started.
.SH SECURITY
.B fsb
is designed so that a web page cannot use it.
It listens only on 127.0.0.1; it refuses requests whose
.B Host
is not exactly the loopback address and port, and any request that carries
another
origin (including a page on another port of the same host).
The session cookie is
.B HttpOnly
and
.BR SameSite=Strict ,
and is scoped to a random path prefix, so that the browser never sends it to
another web
server on 127.0.0.1.
Only
.B GET
and
.B HEAD
are answered.
Files are never rendered from the interface's own origin except as the previews
described above; downloads are always sent as attachments.
.B fsb
makes no outbound connection, sends no telemetry, and never causes a
cloud-only file to
be downloaded.
A denied path is indistinguishable from a missing one.
.PP
Anything that runs as you can read the same files:
.B fsb
is not a sandbox against local malware.
See the security policy shipped with the source for the threat model, the
guarantees and
the known limitations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Browse your home folder:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb
.RE
.PP
Browse one folder, and open it in Chrome:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-browser """Google Chrome""" ~/Documents
.RE
.PP
Serve two folders and paste the URL yourself:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-no\-open \-\-root /Volumes/Data ~/src
.RE
.PP
Use the same port every time, so the browser remembers your preferences:
.PP
.RS
.B fsb \-\-port 4488
.RE
.PP
Write the rule files, then edit them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fsb \-\-init
$EDITOR ~/.config/fsb/deny
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B fsb: refusing to serve / without \-\-allow\-system\-root
Serving the whole system needs the explicit option.
.TP
.B fsb: deny rules: FILE: line N (...): MESSAGE
A rule file could not be parsed, and
.B fsb
did not start.
Fix the line named.
.TP
.B fsb: could not open the browser
The application named by
.B \-\-browser
was not found.
The server is running; open the printed URL yourself.
.TP
.B Not found. This path is outside the folders fsb serves
Shown in the interface for a path outside every root; start
.B fsb
with a different path, or add
.BR \-\-root .
.SH LIMITATIONS
A hard link whose denied name lies outside every root and outside the credential
folders is not detected.
Rule files are read at startup only.
A denied path can be answered slightly faster than a missing one.
.B fsb
targets macOS; the interface is tested in Chrome and Safari.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (1),
.BR gitignore (5)
.PP
The functional and design specifications and the security policy, in the
.I specs
folder of the source.
